var e={level:`B2`,type:`word-bank`,questions:[{id:`b2-wb-001`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Despite' is followed by a noun phrase (the heavy rain).`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' is followed by a clause (subject + verb), not by a noun phrase like 'the heavy rain'.`,however:`'however' links two separate sentences. It is not followed by a noun phrase like this.`,"because of":`'because of' gives a reason. The walk happened in spite of the rain, so we need 'despite'.`},bank:[`despite`,`although`,`however`,`because of`],text:`___ the heavy rain, we went for a walk.`,answers:[`despite`]},{id:`b2-wb-002`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if ... not': I won't go if you don't come.`,wrongReasons:{if:`'I won't go if you come with me' would mean the opposite. We need 'unless' (= if you don't).`,whether:`'whether' introduces a choice or a doubt (I don't know whether he will come), not a condition like this.`,as:`'as' gives a reason or a time, not a condition.`},bank:[`unless`,`if`,`whether`,`as`],text:`I won't go ___ you come with me.`,answers:[`unless`]},{id:`b2-wb-003`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Although' is followed by a full clause (subject + verb): Although she was tired, she kept working.`,wrongReasons:{Despite:`'Despite' must be followed by a noun or an -ing form, not by a clause like 'the museum was crowded'.`,"In spite":`'In spite' is incomplete: we need 'in spite of', and even then it takes a noun, not a clause.`,However:`'However' starts a new sentence or follows a semicolon; it cannot join these two clauses in this way.`},bank:[`Although`,`However`,`Despite`,`In spite`],text:`___ the museum was crowded, we managed to see every painting.`,answers:[`Although`]},{id:`b2-wb-004`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Whereas' contrasts two different facts: My brother loves jazz, whereas I prefer rock.`,wrongReasons:{"so that":`'so that' expresses purpose (in order to), but there is no purpose here, only a contrast.`,"as long as":`'as long as' introduces a condition, but the sentence simply compares two regions.`,"in case":`'in case' means 'as a precaution', which makes no sense when describing geography.`},bank:[`so that`,`whereas`,`as long as`,`in case`],text:`The north of the country is dry and flat, ___ the south is green and mountainous.`,answers:[`whereas`]},{id:`b2-wb-005`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'In case' means 'as a precaution because something might happen': Take a map in case you get lost.`,wrongReasons:{unless:`'unless' means 'if not'. 'Bring a charger unless your battery runs out' gives the wrong meaning.`,"so as":`'so as' needs 'to' + infinitive (so as to avoid), not a subject and verb.`,"even though":`'even though' shows contrast, but bringing a charger is a precaution, not a contrast.`},bank:[`even though`,`in case`,`so as`,`unless`],text:`Bring a charger with you ___ your phone battery runs out during the trip.`,answers:[`in case`]},{id:`b2-wb-006`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Owing to' gives a reason and is followed by a noun: The match was cancelled owing to the storm.`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' introduces a contrast and needs a clause; the fog is a cause, not a contrast.`,so:`'so' introduces a result, not a reason. It cannot come before the cause 'thick fog'.`,"in order to":`'in order to' expresses purpose and is followed by a verb, not a noun like 'fog'.`},bank:[`although`,`owing to`,`so`,`in order to`],text:`All flights to the island were delayed ___ thick fog at the airport.`,answers:[`owing to`]},{id:`b2-wb-007`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Even though' adds a strong contrast and is followed by a clause: She went out even though it was snowing.`,wrongReasons:{despite:`'despite' cannot be followed by a full clause such as 'he had practised'; it needs a noun or -ing.`,because:`'because' gives a reason, but practising every day is not a reason to fail.`,"as a result":`'as a result' introduces a consequence, which is the opposite of the logic here.`},bank:[`as a result`,`even though`,`despite`,`because`],text:`He failed the driving test ___ he had practised every day for a month.`,answers:[`even though`]},{id:`b2-wb-008`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Moreover' adds a further point that supports the first one: The flat is cheap. Moreover, it is close to work.`,wrongReasons:{Nevertheless:`'Nevertheless' shows contrast, but both sentences say positive things about the software.`,Otherwise:`'Otherwise' means 'if not', which does not connect two advantages.`,Instead:`'Instead' introduces an alternative, but here we are adding a second advantage.`},bank:[`Moreover`,`Instead`,`Nevertheless`,`Otherwise`],text:`The new software is easy to use. ___, it costs much less than the old system.`,answers:[`Moreover`]},{id:`b2-wb-009`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'So that' expresses purpose and is followed by a clause, often with can/could: I left early so that I could catch the train.`,wrongReasons:{"in order to":`'in order to' is followed by a base verb, not by a subject + verb like 'the new students could'.`,such:`'such' is used before a noun phrase (such a slow speaker), not to introduce purpose.`,therefore:`'therefore' introduces a logical result, not the aim of an action.`},bank:[`such`,`so that`,`therefore`,`in order to`],text:`The teacher spoke slowly ___ the new students could follow the lesson.`,answers:[`so that`]},{id:`b2-wb-010`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'As long as' means 'only if': You can borrow my car as long as you drive carefully.`,wrongReasons:{whereas:`'whereas' compares two different facts; it does not set a condition.`,although:`'although' shows contrast, but the landlord is giving a condition.`,"in spite of":`'in spite of' is followed by a noun, not a clause, and it shows contrast, not a condition.`},bank:[`as long as`,`in spite of`,`whereas`,`although`],text:`The landlord said we could keep a cat ___ it did not damage the furniture.`,answers:[`as long as`]},{id:`b2-wb-011`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Despite' is followed by a noun or -ing form; 'although' is followed by a clause: Despite the rain, we went out. Although it was raining, we went out.`,wrongReasons:{Although:`'Although' needs a subject and verb after it (although it had a huge budget), not a noun phrase like 'its huge budget'.`,Because:`'Because' needs a clause, and a huge budget is not a reason for failure.`,Unless:`'Unless' introduces a condition (if not), which does not fit a fact about the past.`},bank:[`Because`,`Although`,`Unless`,`Despite`],text:`___ its huge budget, the film was not a success at the box office.`,answers:[`Despite`]},{id:`b2-wb-012`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Whereas' contrasts two facts; 'otherwise' means 'if not': Hurry, otherwise we'll miss the bus.`,wrongReasons:{"0:otherwise":`'otherwise' means 'if not'; it does not compare two people's habits.`,"0:so that":`'so that' expresses purpose; my spending is not the purpose of my sister's saving.`,"0:because of":`'because of' is followed by a noun and gives a reason, not a contrast between two people.`,"1:whereas":`'whereas' compares two facts, but here we need 'if I am not careful', which is 'otherwise'.`,"1:so that":`'so that' would mean that being careful has the aim of never buying a flat, which is illogical.`,"1:because of":`'because of' needs a noun after it, not a full clause like 'I will never buy a flat'.`},bank:[`because of`,`so that`,`whereas`,`otherwise`],text:`My sister saves most of her salary, ___ I spend mine as soon as I get it. I should be careful, ___ I will never buy a flat.`,answers:[`whereas`,`otherwise`]},{id:`b2-wb-013`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Therefore' introduces a logical result: The road was closed; therefore, we took the bus.`,wrongReasons:{although:`'although' shows contrast, but reducing staff is a result of losing the client.`,despite:`'despite' needs a noun after it and shows contrast, not a result.`,whereas:`'whereas' compares two separate facts; it cannot show that one thing caused another.`},bank:[`therefore`,`despite`,`although`,`whereas`],text:`The company lost its biggest client last year and ___ had to reduce its staff.`,answers:[`therefore`]},{id:`b2-wb-014`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Apart from' means 'except for' or 'in addition to' and is followed by a noun or -ing: Apart from the price, I liked everything.`,wrongReasons:{Unless:`'Unless' introduces a condition and needs a clause, not a noun like 'a small scratch'.`,"Even if":`'Even if' introduces a hypothetical condition with a clause, not a noun phrase.`,Whereas:`'Whereas' must join two clauses that contrast; it cannot be followed by a noun phrase alone.`},bank:[`Unless`,`Even if`,`Apart from`,`Whereas`],text:`___ a small scratch on the door, the second-hand car was in perfect condition.`,answers:[`Apart from`]},{id:`b2-wb-015`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'As soon as' means 'immediately after': Call me as soon as you arrive.`,wrongReasons:{so:`'so' introduces a result; the rain stopping did not happen because the children ran.`,"in spite of":`'in spite of' needs a noun or -ing form, not a clause like 'the rain stopped'.`,provided:`'provided' introduces a condition, but this sentence describes a past event in time order.`},bank:[`so`,`provided`,`as soon as`,`in spite of`],text:`The children ran outside ___ the rain stopped.`,answers:[`as soon as`]},{id:`b2-wb-016`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Instead of' + -ing means 'in place of doing': Instead of driving, we walked.`,wrongReasons:{Rather:`'Rather' alone is incomplete here; we would need 'rather than'.`,Despite:`'Despite complaining' means 'although you complain', which does not fit a suggestion to do something different.`,Due:`'Due' needs 'to' after it (due to) and gives a reason, not an alternative action.`},bank:[`Instead of`,`Rather`,`Despite`,`Due`],text:`___ complaining about the noise, why don't you talk to your neighbours?`,answers:[`Instead of`]},{id:`b2-wb-017`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Whose' shows possession in a relative clause: That's the man whose dog bit me.`,wrongReasons:{who:`'who fields' is not possible; we need a possessive word because the fields belong to the farmer.`,which:`'which' refers to things and is not possessive, so 'which fields had been flooded' loses the link to the farmer.`,whom:`'whom' is an object pronoun for people; it cannot show possession.`},bank:[`which`,`whose`,`who`,`whom`],text:`I spoke to a farmer ___ fields had been flooded by the river.`,answers:[`whose`]},{id:`b2-wb-018`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Where' introduces a relative clause about a place: This is the café where we first met.`,wrongReasons:{which:`'which' would need a preposition (in which), because nobody spoke English IN the village.`,when:`'when' refers to a time, but 'a small village' is a place.`,whose:`'whose' shows possession and must be followed by a noun, not by 'nobody'.`},bank:[`which`,`when`,`whose`,`where`],text:`We stayed in a small village ___ nobody spoke English.`,answers:[`where`]},{id:`b2-wb-019`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`In relative clauses between commas (extra information), use 'which' (not 'that') for things: My laptop, which is new, already has a problem.`,wrongReasons:{that:`'that' cannot be used in a relative clause between commas that adds extra information.`,what:`'what' means 'the thing that' and cannot refer back to a noun like 'the Eiffel Tower'.`,who:`'who' is for people, and the Eiffel Tower is a thing.`},bank:[`that`,`what`,`which`,`who`],text:`The Eiffel Tower, ___ was built for a world fair, was not popular at first.`,answers:[`which`]},{id:`b2-wb-020`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'When' introduces a relative clause about a time: I remember the day when we moved house.`,wrongReasons:{where:`'where' refers to a place, but 'the summer' is a period of time.`,which:`'which' would need a preposition here (in which), because we drove IN the summer.`,whose:`'whose' shows possession and must be followed by a noun.`},bank:[`whose`,`where`,`which`,`when`],text:`Do you remember the summer ___ we drove across Spain in that old van?`,answers:[`when`]},{id:`b2-wb-021`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Whom' is the formal object form of 'who', especially after a preposition: the person to whom I wrote.`,wrongReasons:{who:`After a preposition like 'with', formal English uses 'whom', not 'who'.`,which:`'which' is for things, but a scientist is a person.`,whose:`'whose' needs a noun after it (whose office), so 'with whose I shared' is incomplete.`},bank:[`which`,`whom`,`whose`,`who`],text:`The scientist with ___ I shared an office later won a major prize.`,answers:[`whom`]},{id:`b2-wb-022`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'What' means 'the thing(s) that' and has no noun before it: What I need is a holiday.`,wrongReasons:{Which:`'Which' needs a noun before it to refer to; here there is no noun, so we need 'What' (= the thing that).`,That:`'That surprised me most' would be a separate sentence; it cannot be the subject of 'was'.`,Who:`'Who' refers to a person, but the thing that surprised me was a quality of the city.`},bank:[`That`,`Which`,`Who`,`What`],text:`___ surprised me most about the city was how friendly the people were.`,answers:[`What`]},{id:`b2-wb-023`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Which' can refer to a whole previous clause: He arrived late, which annoyed everyone.`,wrongReasons:{what:`'what' cannot refer back to a previous clause after a comma.`,that:`'that' cannot be used after a comma to refer to a whole clause.`,it:`'it' would create two sentences joined only by a comma, which is a punctuation error.`},bank:[`what`,`that`,`it`,`which`],text:`The train was cancelled at the last minute, ___ meant we had to take a taxi.`,answers:[`which`]},{id:`b2-wb-024`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Who' refers to people as the subject of a relative clause: The woman who lives next door is a nurse.`,wrongReasons:{whose:`'whose' must be followed by a noun (whose boots), not directly by a verb.`,which:`'which' is for things and animals, not for players.`,where:`'where' refers to a place, but 'players' are people.`},bank:[`where`,`whose`,`which`,`who`],text:`Players ___ arrive late for training will not be selected for Saturday's match.`,answers:[`who`]},{id:`b2-wb-025`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`After 'all', 'everything' or 'nothing' we use 'that' (not 'what'): All that matters is your health.`,wrongReasons:{what:`'what' already contains 'the thing that', so 'everything what' repeats the idea and is wrong.`,who:`'who' refers to people, but 'everything' refers to things.`,whose:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it.`},bank:[`who`,`that`,`whose`,`what`],text:`Everything ___ the witness told the police turned out to be true.`,answers:[`that`]},{id:`b2-wb-026`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Whose' shows possession; 'which' can follow a preposition: the house in which I grew up.`,wrongReasons:{"0:which":`'which' does not show possession; we need 'whose' because the novel belongs to the author.`,"0:who":`'who novel' is impossible; 'who' cannot be followed directly by a noun to show possession.`,"0:where":`'where' refers to a place, but here we need possession: the author's novel.`,"1:whose":`'whose' must be followed by a noun; it cannot stand alone before 'more than'.`,"1:which":`'which' would need a preposition (in which) because the people will be IN the library.`,"1:who":`'who' refers to people, but here the clause describes the library, a place.`},bank:[`where`,`who`,`which`,`whose`],text:`The author ___ novel won the award is giving a talk at the city library, ___ more than two hundred people are expected.`,answers:[`whose`,`where`]},{id:`b2-wb-027`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Of whom' and 'of which' are used after quantity words in relative clauses after a comma: I have two cousins, both of whom are doctors.`,wrongReasons:{them:`'all of them' would need a new sentence or a linking word; after a comma we need a relative pronoun.`,who:`After a preposition like 'of', we use 'whom', not 'who'.`,which:`'which' is for things, but brothers are people.`},bank:[`them`,`whom`,`which`,`who`],text:`She has three brothers, all of ___ work in the family business.`,answers:[`whom`]},{id:`b2-wb-028`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Where' replaces 'in which' for places: the factory where (= in which) my father worked.`,wrongReasons:{that:`'that I was born' is missing a preposition; we would need 'that I was born in'.`,who:`'who' refers to people, not to a building.`,when:`'when' refers to a time, but 'the hospital' is a place.`},bank:[`where`,`when`,`who`,`that`],text:`The hospital ___ I was born was knocked down last year.`,answers:[`where`]},{id:`b2-wb-029`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Use 'which' for things in a defining clause after a preposition: the reason for which he resigned.`,wrongReasons:{whom:`'whom' is for people, but 'the project' is a thing.`,that:`'that' cannot come directly after a preposition such as 'on'.`,what:`'what' cannot follow a noun and a preposition to refer back to 'the project'.`},bank:[`what`,`that`,`which`,`whom`],text:`The project on ___ she spent three years has finally been published.`,answers:[`which`]},{id:`b2-wb-030`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Why' introduces a relative clause after 'the reason': That's the reason why I left.`,wrongReasons:{which:`'the reason which the manager resigned' needs a preposition (for which); alone it is wrong.`,where:`'where' refers to a place, not to a reason.`,whose:`'whose' needs a noun after it to show possession.`},bank:[`whose`,`where`,`which`,`why`],text:`Nobody understood the reason ___ the manager suddenly resigned.`,answers:[`why`]},{id:`b2-wb-031`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`The third conditional uses 'if' + past perfect and 'would have' + past participle: If I had known, I would have helped.`,wrongReasons:{would:`'would' is not used in the if-clause of a third conditional; we need the past perfect (had left).`,have:`'we have left' is present perfect, which cannot describe an unreal past situation.`,did:`'did left' is not a correct verb form; the past perfect is 'had left'.`},bank:[`had`,`have`,`did`,`would`],text:`If we ___ left ten minutes earlier, we wouldn't have missed the ferry.`,answers:[`had`]},{id:`b2-wb-032`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`The second conditional uses 'would' + base verb in the main clause: If I had more time, I would learn Japanese.`,wrongReasons:{will:`'will' belongs to the first conditional; with 'If I lived' (unreal present) we need 'would'.`,had:`'had cycle' is not a correct verb form.`,am:`'am cycle' is impossible; 'am' cannot be followed by a base verb.`},bank:[`had`,`would`,`will`,`am`],text:`If I lived closer to the office, I ___ cycle to work every day.`,answers:[`would`]},{id:`b2-wb-033`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if ... not': You won't pass unless you study (= if you don't study).`,wrongReasons:{If:`'If the government acts quickly, many shops will close' gives the opposite meaning; quick action should save the shops.`,Provided:`'Provided' sets a positive condition, so it would also mean that quick action causes the shops to close.`,"As long as":`'As long as' means 'only if', which again would make quick action the cause of the closures.`},bank:[`If`,`As long as`,`Unless`,`Provided`],text:`___ the government acts quickly, many small shops will close this year.`,answers:[`Unless`]},{id:`b2-wb-034`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A mixed conditional can link a past condition to a present result: If I had taken the job, I would be rich now.`,wrongReasons:{"have been":`'would have been living in London now' mixes a past form with 'now'; for a present result we use 'would be'.`,being:`'would being' is not possible; 'would' is followed by a base verb.`,been:`'would been' is wrong; 'been' needs 'have' before it.`},bank:[`have been`,`being`,`be`,`been`],text:`If she had accepted the offer from the bank, she would ___ living in London now.`,answers:[`be`]},{id:`b2-wb-035`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Provided (that)' means 'only if': You can come provided that you bring some food.`,wrongReasons:{unless:`'unless' means 'if not', so the sentence would say the bank lends money only to people without an income.`,otherwise:`'otherwise' means 'if not' and usually starts a new clause after a comma; it cannot introduce this condition.`,whether:`'whether' introduces a choice between possibilities, not a requirement for the loan.`},bank:[`unless`,`provided`,`whether`,`otherwise`],text:`The bank will lend you the money ___ you can show a regular income.`,answers:[`provided`]},{id:`b2-wb-036`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Were' is used for all persons in formal second conditionals: If I were you, I would apologise.`,wrongReasons:{am:`'am' is present simple; an imaginary situation in the second conditional needs a past form (were).`,"would be":`'would' is not used in the if-clause; it belongs to the main clause.`,"have been":`'have been' is present perfect and does not express an unreal present situation.`},bank:[`were`,`would be`,`am`,`have been`],text:`If I ___ in your position, I would ask for a second opinion.`,answers:[`were`]},{id:`b2-wb-037`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`In a third conditional, the main clause uses 'would/could/might have' + past participle: If he had trained, he could have won.`,wrongReasons:{can:`'can have won' is not used for an unreal past result; we need 'could have'.`,will:`'will have won' is a future perfect form; it does not express an imagined past result.`,had:`'had have won' is not a correct verb form.`},bank:[`could`,`had`,`can`,`will`],text:`If the referee had seen the foul, our team ___ have won the match.`,answers:[`could`]},{id:`b2-wb-038`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`The zero conditional uses the present simple in both clauses for general truths: If you heat ice, it melts.`,wrongReasons:{"would boil":`'would boil' is for imaginary situations, but this is a scientific fact.`,boiled:`'boiled' is past simple; general truths use the present simple.`,"will have boiled":`'will have boiled' looks at a completed future action, not at a general truth.`},bank:[`would boil`,`boils`,`boiled`,`will have boiled`],text:`If you heat water to 100 degrees at sea level, it ___.`,answers:[`boils`]},{id:`b2-wb-039`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Should' at the start of a clause is a formal way of saying 'if': Should you need help, call me (= if you need help).`,wrongReasons:{Would:`'Would you have any questions' is a question form, not a conditional; it does not mean 'if'.`,Had:`'Had you have' is incorrect; inverted 'had' needs a past participle (had you had).`,Were:`'Were you have' is wrong; inverted 'were' needs 'to' + infinitive (were you to have).`},bank:[`Should`,`Had`,`Would`,`Were`],text:`___ you have any questions about your booking, please contact our office.`,answers:[`Should`]},{id:`b2-wb-040`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Otherwise' means 'if not': Wear a coat, otherwise you'll get cold.`,wrongReasons:{unless:`'unless' must be followed by a clause with a subject and verb, not by a comma.`,if:`'if' cannot stand alone between commas; it needs a condition clause after it.`,provided:`'provided' introduces a condition clause and cannot be used alone before a comma.`},bank:[`unless`,`otherwise`,`provided`,`if`],text:`You need to book a table in advance; ___, you might not get a seat on a Saturday night.`,answers:[`otherwise`]},{id:`b2-wb-041`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + base verb. 'Unless' means 'if not': If it rains, we'll stay in. We'll go unless it rains.`,wrongReasons:{"0:will":`'will' is not used in the if-clause of a first conditional; we need the present simple (rains).`,"0:unless":`'unless' is a conjunction, not a verb, so 'If it unless' makes no sense.`,"0:would":`'it would' needs a base verb after it, and 'would' does not belong in this if-clause.`,"1:rains":`'rains' is a verb, but the gap needs an auxiliary before 'be moved'.`,"1:unless":`'unless' cannot be used before 'be moved'; we need a modal auxiliary.`,"1:would":`'would' is for unreal situations; with 'If it rains' (real possibility) we need 'will'.`},bank:[`unless`,`would`,`will`,`rains`],text:`If it ___ tomorrow, the outdoor concert ___ be moved to the sports hall.`,answers:[`rains`,`will`]},{id:`b2-wb-042`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Wish' / 'if only' + past perfect express regret about the past: I wish I hadn't said that.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't":`'didn't deleted' is incorrect; 'didn't' must be followed by a base verb, and regret about the past needs 'hadn't'.`,"wouldn't":`'wouldn't deleted' is incorrect; 'would' needs a base verb, and it does not express past regret.`,"haven't":`'haven't deleted' is present perfect, which is not used after 'if only' for past regret.`},bank:[`didn't`,`wouldn't`,`haven't`,`hadn't`],text:`If only I ___ deleted that file; now I have to write the whole report again.`,answers:[`hadn't`]},{id:`b2-wb-043`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Suppose' or 'supposing' introduces an imaginary situation, often with a past tense: Suppose you won the lottery, what would you do?`,wrongReasons:{Unless:`'Unless you lost your passport, what would you do' does not make sense; 'unless' means 'if not'.`,Although:`'Although' introduces a contrast with a real fact, not an imaginary situation followed by a question.`,Despite:`'Despite' cannot be followed by a clause like 'you lost your passport'.`},bank:[`Although`,`Unless`,`Despite`,`Suppose`],text:`___ you lost your passport abroad, what would you do first?`,answers:[`Suppose`]},{id:`b2-wb-044`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Even if' means 'whether or not this happens, the result is the same': I'll go even if it rains.`,wrongReasons:{unless:`'unless' (= if not) would mean I will finish only if I don't have to walk, which is the wrong idea.`,"as long as":`'as long as' makes walking a requirement for finishing, which is not the speaker's meaning.`,"so that":`'so that' expresses purpose; walking is not the aim of finishing the race.`},bank:[`unless`,`even if`,`as long as`,`so that`],text:`I'm going to finish this marathon ___ I have to walk the last ten kilometres.`,answers:[`even if`]},{id:`b2-wb-045`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Put off' means 'postpone': They put off the meeting until Friday.`,wrongReasons:{"put up":`'put up' means 'build' or 'raise' (put up a tent), not 'postpone'.`,"put on":`'put on' means 'wear' or 'organise a show', which does not fit a decision.`,"put out":`'put out' means 'stop a fire' or 'inconvenience someone', not 'delay'.`},bank:[`put off`,`put on`,`put out`,`put up`],text:`The council has ___ the decision about the new bridge until next spring.`,answers:[`put off`]},{id:`b2-wb-046`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Come across' means 'find by chance': I came across some old letters in the attic.`,wrongReasons:{"came up":`'came up' means 'was mentioned' or 'appeared'; it does not take an object like 'a box'.`,"came round":`'came round' means 'visited' or 'became conscious again', not 'found'.`,"came off":`'came off' means 'became detached' or 'succeeded', which makes no sense with a box of photos.`},bank:[`came across`,`came off`,`came round`,`came up`],text:`While cleaning the garage, I ___ a box of photos from my grandparents' wedding.`,answers:[`came across`]},{id:`b2-wb-047`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Run out of' means 'have no more of something': We've run out of milk.`,wrongReasons:{"run into":`'run into' means 'meet by chance' or 'hit', which does not fit 'petrol'.`,"run over":`'run over' means 'hit with a vehicle' or 'go past a time limit'.`,"run after":`'run after' means 'chase', which makes no sense with petrol.`},bank:[`run out of`,`run after`,`run into`,`run over`],text:`We had to stop at a small village because we had ___ petrol.`,answers:[`run out of`]},{id:`b2-wb-048`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Turn down' means 'refuse an offer': She turned down the job because the salary was low.`,wrongReasons:{"turned up":`'turned up' means 'arrived' or 'increased the volume', not 'refused'.`,"turned into":`'turned into' means 'became', which does not fit an offer.`,"turned over":`'turned over' means 'moved onto the other side' or 'gave control', not 'refused'.`},bank:[`turned up`,`turned over`,`turned into`,`turned down`],text:`He ___ the offer of a promotion because it meant moving to another city.`,answers:[`turned down`]},{id:`b2-wb-049`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Look into' means 'investigate': The police are looking into the robbery.`,wrongReasons:{"look after":`'look after' means 'take care of' a person or thing, not 'investigate' a complaint.`,"look up to":`'look up to' means 'admire', which does not fit a complaint.`,"look forward to":`'look forward to' means 'feel happy about something in the future', which is illogical with a complaint.`},bank:[`look after`,`look up to`,`look forward to`,`look into`],text:`The manager promised to ___ the customer's complaint and reply within a week.`,answers:[`look into`]},{id:`b2-wb-050`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Get over' means 'recover from' an illness or a bad experience: It took her months to get over the flu.`,wrongReasons:{"get away":`'get away' means 'escape' or 'go on holiday'; it does not take an object like 'the disappointment'.`,"get by":`'get by' means 'manage with little money or knowledge', not 'recover'.`,"get through to":`'get through to' means 'make someone understand' or 'contact by phone'.`},bank:[`get through to`,`get away`,`get over`,`get by`],text:`It took the team a long time to ___ the disappointment of losing the final.`,answers:[`get over`]},{id:`b2-wb-051`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Set up' means 'start or establish' a business or organisation: She set up her own company at 25.`,wrongReasons:{"set off":`'set off' means 'start a journey' or 'make an alarm ring', not 'establish'.`,"set back":`'set back' means 'delay progress', which is not what the students did.`,"set aside":`'set aside' means 'save for a purpose' (set aside money), not 'found an organisation'.`},bank:[`set up`,`set back`,`set off`,`set aside`],text:`Two former students ___ a charity that provides free laptops to schools.`,answers:[`set up`]},{id:`b2-wb-052`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Take up' means 'start a new hobby or activity': He took up yoga last year.`,wrongReasons:{"took off":`'took off' means 'removed clothes' or 'left the ground' (a plane), not 'started a hobby'.`,"took over":`'took over' means 'took control of', for example a company.`,"took after":`'took after' means 'resembled an older relative', which does not fit 'painting'.`},bank:[`took after`,`took off`,`took over`,`took up`],text:`After retiring, my father ___ painting and now sells his work at local markets.`,answers:[`took up`]},{id:`b2-wb-053`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Break down' means 'stop working' (a machine) and 'carry on' means 'continue': The lift broke down. Carry on working.`,wrongReasons:{"0:carry on":`'Our coach carry on' is wrong: the subject needs a past verb, and the coach clearly stopped.`,"0:broke up":`'broke up' means 'ended a relationship' or 'ended a school term', not 'stopped working'.`,"0:carry out":`'carry out' means 'perform a task' and needs an object; it does not describe a coach stopping.`,"1:broke down":`'had to broke down' is wrong; after 'had to' we need a base verb, and the meaning is 'continue'.`,"1:broke up":`'broke up' is a past form, and after 'had to' we need a base verb.`,"1:carry out":`'carry out' needs an object (carry out a plan); it does not simply mean 'continue'.`},bank:[`broke down`,`carry out`,`carry on`,`broke up`],text:`Our coach ___ halfway up the mountain, so we had to ___ on foot.`,answers:[`broke down`,`carry on`]},{id:`b2-wb-054`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Make up' can mean 'invent' a story or excuse: He made up an excuse for being late.`,wrongReasons:{"made out":`'made out' means 'managed to see or understand' something with difficulty.`,"made for":`'made for' means 'moved towards' a place, not 'invented'.`,"made off":`'made off' means 'escaped quickly', and it needs 'with' before an object.`},bank:[`made up`,`made off`,`made for`,`made out`],text:`The boy ___ a story about a dog eating his homework, but the teacher didn't believe him.`,answers:[`made up`]},{id:`b2-wb-055`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Bring up' can mean 'raise a child' or 'mention a topic': She brought up the issue at the meeting.`,wrongReasons:{"brought about":`'brought about' means 'caused' a change, not 'mentioned' a topic.`,"brought back":`'brought back' means 'returned' something or 'reminded of the past'.`,"brought down":`'brought down' means 'made a government fall' or 'reduced', not 'mentioned'.`},bank:[`brought down`,`brought up`,`brought about`,`brought back`],text:`During the meeting, one of the nurses ___ the problem of staff shortages.`,answers:[`brought up`]},{id:`b2-wb-056`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Give up' means 'stop doing something' or 'stop trying': She gave up smoking ten years ago.`,wrongReasons:{"give away":`'give away' means 'give something for free' or 'reveal a secret'; it needs an object.`,"give off":`'give off' means 'produce heat, light or a smell', which does not describe climbers.`,"give out":`'give out' means 'distribute' or 'stop working' (my legs gave out), not 'stop trying'.`},bank:[`give off`,`give away`,`give up`,`give out`],text:`The climbers refused to ___ even when the weather became dangerous.`,answers:[`give up`]},{id:`b2-wb-057`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Point out' means 'tell someone something they had not noticed': She pointed out a mistake in my report.`,wrongReasons:{"pointed at":`'pointed at' means 'showed with a finger' and is followed by a thing or person, not a 'that' clause.`,"handed out":`'handed out' means 'distributed', which cannot be followed by 'that'.`,"carried out":`'carried out' means 'performed a task' (carried out a survey) and cannot be followed by a 'that' clause.`},bank:[`carried out`,`handed out`,`pointed out`,`pointed at`],text:`My editor ___ that the dates in the second chapter were wrong.`,answers:[`pointed out`]},{id:`b2-wb-058`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Fall out with' means 'argue and stop being friends': He fell out with his brother over money.`,wrongReasons:{"fell for":`'fell for' means 'fell in love with' or 'was tricked by'; it is not followed by 'with'.`,"fell behind":`'fell behind' means 'did not keep up' with work or other people.`,"fell through":`'fell through' means that a plan failed; it does not describe an argument.`},bank:[`fell out`,`fell through`,`fell behind`,`fell for`],text:`The two neighbours ___ with each other over a tree that blocked the light.`,answers:[`fell out`]},{id:`b2-wb-059`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`We 'make a decision' (not 'do a decision'): We need to make a decision by Monday.`,wrongReasons:{do:`We do not say 'do a decision'; 'do' goes with tasks and work (do the homework).`,get:`'get a decision' is not a natural collocation; the committee itself decides, so it 'makes' a decision.`,have:`'have a decision' is not a natural collocation in English.`},bank:[`have`,`do`,`get`,`make`],text:`The committee will ___ a final decision about the budget next week.`,answers:[`make`]},{id:`b2-wb-060`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Heavy traffic' is the natural collocation for a lot of cars on the road: We were late because of heavy traffic.`,wrongReasons:{strong:`'strong' collocates with wind, coffee or opinions, not with traffic.`,big:`'big traffic' is not natural English; we describe a large amount of traffic as 'heavy'.`,thick:`'thick' goes with fog, smoke or a book, not with traffic.`},bank:[`thick`,`heavy`,`big`,`strong`],text:`We were stuck in ___ traffic for two hours on the way to the airport.`,answers:[`heavy`]},{id:`b2-wb-061`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Affect' is a verb (to influence); 'effect' is usually a noun (a result): The weather affected our plans.`,wrongReasons:{effected:`'effected' is a rare formal verb meaning 'caused to happen'; the usual verb for 'influenced' is 'affected'.`,infected:`'infected' is used for diseases and viruses, not for a strike.`,afflicted:`'afflicted' means 'caused serious suffering', usually by illness; it is too strong and unusual here.`},bank:[`affected`,`effected`,`infected`,`afflicted`],text:`The strike ___ thousands of passengers who were trying to travel home for the holidays.`,answers:[`affected`]},{id:`b2-wb-062`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Raise' needs an object (raise prices, raise your hand); 'rise' has no object (prices rise).`,wrongReasons:{rose:`'rose' is the past simple of 'rise' and cannot follow 'has'; also 'rise' takes no object.`,risen:`'risen' is the past participle of 'rise', which has no object, so it cannot take 'the rent'.`,arose:`'arose' means 'appeared' (a problem arose) and is a past simple form, not a participle.`},bank:[`risen`,`rose`,`arose`,`raised`],text:`The landlord has ___ the rent by fifteen per cent this year.`,answers:[`raised`]},{id:`b2-wb-063`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Remind' means 'make someone remember'; 'remember' means 'keep in your memory': Remind me to call Tom.`,wrongReasons:{remember:`'remember me to buy' is not the right pattern; 'remember' is what you do, 'remind' is what you make someone else do.`,recall:`'recall' means 'remember something from the past' and is not followed by 'me to'.`,memorise:`'memorise' means 'learn by heart', which does not fit buying stamps.`},bank:[`remember`,`memorise`,`remind`,`recall`],text:`Could you ___ me to buy some stamps when we pass the post office?`,answers:[`remind`]},{id:`b2-wb-064`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Lend' means 'give for a short time'; 'borrow' means 'take for a short time': Can you lend me your pen?`,wrongReasons:{borrow:`We say 'borrow something FROM someone'; 'borrow me your camera' is wrong.`,owe:`'owe' means 'need to pay back a debt', which does not fit the request.`,rent:`'rent' involves paying money, but here the speaker is asking a favour.`},bank:[`lend`,`owe`,`borrow`,`rent`],text:`Could you ___ me your camera for the weekend? I'll give it back on Monday.`,answers:[`lend`]},{id:`b2-wb-065`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Economical' means 'saving money or fuel'; 'economic' means 'related to the economy': an economical car.`,wrongReasons:{economic:`'economic' describes the economy (economic growth), not something that saves money.`,economy:`'economy' is a noun; after 'very' we need an adjective.`,economist:`'economist' is a person who studies the economy, not a quality of a car.`},bank:[`economical`,`economy`,`economic`,`economist`],text:`This small car is very ___ because it uses very little fuel.`,answers:[`economical`]},{id:`b2-wb-066`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Sensible' means 'practical and reasonable'; 'sensitive' means 'easily upset or affected': a sensible decision.`,wrongReasons:{sensitive:`'sensitive' means 'easily hurt or affected' (sensitive skin), not 'reasonable'.`,sensational:`'sensational' means 'very exciting or shocking', which does not fit wearing a helmet.`,senseless:`'senseless' means 'without meaning or purpose', the opposite of the idea here.`},bank:[`sensible`,`sensational`,`senseless`,`sensitive`],text:`Wearing a helmet when you ride a bike is the ___ thing to do.`,answers:[`sensible`]},{id:`b2-wb-067`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Pay attention to' is a fixed collocation: Please pay attention to the instructions.`,wrongReasons:{take:`'take attention' is not an English collocation; we 'take notice of' but 'pay attention to'.`,make:`'make attention' is not an English collocation.`,do:`'do attention' is not an English collocation.`},bank:[`pay`,`make`,`do`,`take`],text:`Drivers should ___ attention to road signs, especially in unfamiliar areas.`,answers:[`pay`]},{id:`b2-wb-068`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`We 'achieve' an ambition and 'reach' a verdict: She achieved her ambition. The jury reached a verdict.`,wrongReasons:{"0:reached":`We don't usually say 'reach an ambition'; ambitions are 'achieved' or 'realised'.`,"0:won":`'won' goes with prizes, races and competitions, not with 'her ambition'.`,"0:earned":`'earned' goes with money or respect, not with a personal ambition.`,"1:achieved":`We don't say 'achieve a verdict'; a jury 'reaches' a verdict.`,"1:won":`You cannot 'win a verdict'; 'win' goes with the case, not with the jury's decision.`,"1:earned":`'earned a verdict' is not natural; we 'earn' money or trust.`},bank:[`earned`,`achieved`,`won`,`reached`],text:`After months of training, she finally ___ her ambition of running a marathon. The jury ___ a verdict after two days of discussion.`,answers:[`achieved`,`reached`]},{id:`b2-wb-069`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Convince' means 'make someone believe something': She convinced me that she was telling the truth.`,wrongReasons:{insisted:`'insisted' is followed by 'that' or 'on', not directly by a person like 'the jury'.`,suggested:`'suggested' is not followed directly by a person; we say 'suggested to the jury that...'.`,explained:`'explained' needs 'to' before the person (explained to the jury).`},bank:[`convinced`,`insisted`,`suggested`,`explained`],text:`The lawyer ___ the jury that her client was innocent.`,answers:[`convinced`]},{id:`b2-wb-070`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A 'deadline' is the time by which something must be finished: The deadline for the essay is Friday.`,wrongReasons:{timetable:`A 'timetable' is a list of times for trains, buses or lessons, not a final time limit.`,schedule:`A 'schedule' is a plan of activities over time, not one final limit.`,period:`A 'period' is a length of time, not a single moment by which something must be done.`},bank:[`timetable`,`schedule`,`period`,`deadline`],text:`The ___ for applications is midnight on 30 June, so don't leave it too late.`,answers:[`deadline`]},{id:`b2-wb-071`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Do research' is the usual collocation: She is doing research into ocean pollution.`,wrongReasons:{making:`'make research' is a common learner mistake; we say 'do research'.`,taking:`'take research' is not an English collocation.`,having:`'have research' is not used to mean 'carry out research'.`},bank:[`taking`,`making`,`doing`,`having`],text:`The university is ___ research into new treatments for heart disease.`,answers:[`doing`]},{id:`b2-wb-072`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Journey' is the act of travelling from one place to another; 'trip' is a short visit; 'travel' is usually a verb or uncountable noun.`,wrongReasons:{travel:`'travel' is usually an uncountable noun or a verb; we don't say 'the travel to work'.`,voyage:`A 'voyage' is a long journey by sea or into space, not a daily trip to work.`,excursion:`An 'excursion' is a short trip for pleasure, often in a group, not a daily commute.`},bank:[`excursion`,`travel`,`journey`,`voyage`],text:`The ___ to work takes me about forty minutes by train.`,answers:[`journey`]},{id:`b2-wb-073`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Avoid' is followed by the -ing form: She avoids driving at night.`,wrongReasons:{"to make":`'avoid' is never followed by 'to' + infinitive.`,make:`'avoid' cannot be followed by a bare base verb.`,made:`'made' is a past form and cannot follow 'avoid'.`},bank:[`made`,`make`,`making`,`to make`],text:`Try to avoid ___ the same mistake twice in your essay.`,answers:[`making`]},{id:`b2-wb-074`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Refuse' is followed by 'to' + infinitive: He refused to answer the question.`,wrongReasons:{paying:`'refuse' is not followed by the -ing form.`,pay:`'refuse' needs 'to' before the verb; 'refused pay' is wrong.`,paid:`'paid' is a past form and cannot follow 'refused'.`},bank:[`paying`,`pay`,`paid`,`to pay`],text:`The customer refused ___ for the meal because it was cold.`,answers:[`to pay`]},{id:`b2-wb-075`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Stop + -ing' means 'no longer do'; 'stop + to' means 'stop in order to do': He stopped smoking. He stopped to buy a paper.`,wrongReasons:{checking:`'stopped checking' would mean we no longer checked the tyres; here we stopped the car in order to check them.`,check:`'stopped check' is not correct; 'stop' needs 'to' or -ing.`,checked:`'stopped checked' has two past verbs together, which is wrong.`},bank:[`check`,`to check`,`checked`,`checking`],text:`On the way to the coast, we stopped ___ the tyres at a petrol station.`,answers:[`to check`]},{id:`b2-wb-076`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Remember + -ing' refers to a memory of the past: I remember meeting her at a party in 2015.`,wrongReasons:{"to visit":`'remember to visit' refers to a future duty (don't forget), not to a memory of the past.`,visit:`'remember visit' is wrong; 'remember' needs -ing or 'to' before another verb.`,visited:`'remember visited' is wrong; two finite verbs cannot follow each other like this.`},bank:[`visit`,`visiting`,`to visit`,`visited`],text:`I clearly remember ___ that castle with my parents when I was six.`,answers:[`visiting`]},{id:`b2-wb-077`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Make' + object + base verb (no 'to'): The film made me cry.`,wrongReasons:{"to wait":`In the active voice, 'make someone' is followed by a base verb without 'to'.`,waiting:`'make someone' is not followed by the -ing form.`,waited:`'made us waited' is wrong; after 'make + object' we use the base verb.`},bank:[`wait`,`waiting`,`to wait`,`waited`],text:`The doctor made us ___ for over an hour before she saw us.`,answers:[`wait`]},{id:`b2-wb-078`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Look forward to' is followed by -ing because 'to' is a preposition: I look forward to hearing from you.`,wrongReasons:{see:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so it must be followed by -ing, not a base verb.`,"to see":`'looking forward to to see' repeats 'to' and uses the wrong form.`,saw:`'saw' is a past form and cannot follow a preposition.`},bank:[`see`,`saw`,`seeing`,`to see`],text:`We are really looking forward to ___ you at the wedding in June.`,answers:[`seeing`]},{id:`b2-wb-079`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Want' + object + 'to' + infinitive: My parents want me to study medicine.`,wrongReasons:{come:`'want someone' must be followed by 'to' + infinitive, not a bare verb.`,coming:`'want me coming' is not the standard pattern; we use 'want someone to do'.`,"that she comes":`'want' is not followed by a 'that' clause, and 'she' does not match 'me'.`},bank:[`come`,`that she comes`,`coming`,`to come`],text:`My boss wants me ___ to the meeting in Berlin next week.`,answers:[`to come`]},{id:`b2-wb-080`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Suggest' is followed by -ing or a 'that' clause, never by 'to' + infinitive: He suggested taking a taxi.`,wrongReasons:{"to take":`'suggest' is not followed by 'to' + infinitive.`,take:`'suggested take' is wrong; 'suggest' needs -ing or a 'that' clause.`,"us to take":`'suggest someone to do' is a common mistake; we say 'suggested that we take' or 'suggested taking'.`},bank:[`take`,`taking`,`to take`,`us to take`],text:`Our guide suggested ___ the cable car to the top of the hill.`,answers:[`taking`]},{id:`b2-wb-081`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Let' + object + base verb: My parents let me stay up late.`,wrongReasons:{"to use":`'let someone' is followed by the base verb without 'to'.`,using:`'let someone' is not followed by the -ing form.`,used:`'let the students used' is wrong; after 'let + object' we need the base form.`},bank:[`using`,`use`,`used`,`to use`],text:`The teacher let the students ___ dictionaries during the exam.`,answers:[`use`]},{id:`b2-wb-082`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Manage' takes 'to' + infinitive; 'mind' takes -ing: We managed to finish. Do you mind waiting?`,wrongReasons:{"0:waiting":`'manage' is followed by 'to' + infinitive, and 'waiting a parking space' makes no sense.`,"0:finding":`'manage' is not followed by the -ing form; we say 'managed to find'.`,"0:to wait":`'managed to wait a parking space' is meaningless; 'wait' needs 'for'.`,"1:to find":`'mind' is not followed by 'to' + infinitive, and 'find in the car' does not fit.`,"1:finding":`'finding in the car' has no object and does not fit the request to wait.`,"1:to wait":`'mind' is followed by the -ing form, so 'mind to wait' is wrong.`},bank:[`finding`,`to find`,`to wait`,`waiting`],text:`We finally managed ___ a parking space. Would you mind ___ in the car while I get the tickets?`,answers:[`to find`,`waiting`]},{id:`b2-wb-083`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Be used to' + -ing means 'be accustomed to': She is used to working at night.`,wrongReasons:{get:`'is used to get' is wrong; in 'be used to', 'to' is a preposition and needs -ing.`,"to get":`'used to to get' repeats 'to' and uses the wrong form.`,got:`'got' is a past form and cannot follow 'to' here.`},bank:[`get`,`to get`,`got`,`getting`],text:`After ten years on the farm, he is used to ___ up at five o'clock every morning.`,answers:[`getting`]},{id:`b2-wb-084`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Regret + -ing' means being sorry about something you did in the past: I regret leaving school so early.`,wrongReasons:{"to sell":`'regret to' is used for giving bad news (We regret to inform you), not for a past action you are sorry about.`,sell:`'regretted sell' is wrong; 'regret' needs -ing or 'to' before another verb.`,sold:`'regretted sold' puts two past verbs together, which is wrong.`},bank:[`sold`,`sell`,`to sell`,`selling`],text:`My grandfather always regretted ___ his old motorbike in the 1970s.`,answers:[`selling`]},{id:`b2-wb-085`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Can't help' + -ing means 'be unable to stop yourself': I can't help laughing when I see that video.`,wrongReasons:{"to worry":`In the meaning 'unable to stop', 'can't help' is followed by -ing, not 'to'.`,worry:`'can't help worry' is wrong; the verb after 'can't help' must be in the -ing form.`,worried:`'can't help worried' is wrong; we need the -ing form after 'can't help'.`},bank:[`worrying`,`worried`,`worry`,`to worry`],text:`I know the exam went well, but I can't help ___ about the results.`,answers:[`worrying`]},{id:`b2-wb-086`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Allow' + object + 'to' + infinitive: The museum doesn't allow visitors to take photos.`,wrongReasons:{bring:`'allow someone' needs 'to' before the verb, unlike 'let'.`,bringing:`'allow fans bringing' is wrong; with an object, 'allow' takes 'to' + infinitive.`,brought:`'allow fans brought' is wrong; we need 'to' + base verb.`},bank:[`bringing`,`brought`,`to bring`,`bring`],text:`The stadium does not allow fans ___ glass bottles inside.`,answers:[`to bring`]},{id:`b2-wb-087`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Must have' + past participle expresses a strong deduction about the past: The ground is wet; it must have rained.`,wrongReasons:{"can't":`'can't have gone out' would mean we are sure they did NOT go out, but the evidence suggests they did.`,should:`'should have gone out' expresses advice or criticism about the past, not a deduction.`,would:`'would have gone out' is used in conditionals, not for a logical conclusion.`},bank:[`can't`,`must`,`would`,`should`],text:`The lights are off and nobody is answering. They ___ have gone out.`,answers:[`must`]},{id:`b2-wb-088`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Can't have' + past participle means we are sure something did not happen: He can't have seen me; he didn't wave.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must have read' would mean we are sure she read it, but she has been busy in a meeting.`,might:`'might have read' shows possibility, but the evidence makes it nearly impossible.`,"had to":`'had to have read' expresses obligation, not deduction, and is not natural here.`},bank:[`can't`,`must`,`had to`,`might`],text:`She ___ have read the email yet; she has been in a meeting all morning.`,answers:[`can't`]},{id:`b2-wb-089`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Should have' + past participle criticises a past action: You should have told me earlier.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must have checked' would be a deduction (I'm sure you checked), not a criticism.`,can:`'can have checked' is not used to express past criticism.`,will:`'will have checked' is the future perfect, which does not express criticism.`},bank:[`should`,`must`,`can`,`will`],text:`You ___ have checked the weather forecast before you went sailing.`,answers:[`should`]},{id:`b2-wb-090`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Don't have to' means 'it is not necessary': You don't have to come if you're busy.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't' means 'it is forbidden', but the speaker says you can bring food if you want.`,"can't":`'can't' means 'it is not possible or allowed', which contradicts 'you can if you want'.`,"shouldn't":`'shouldn't' gives advice against doing something, which does not match 'you can if you want'.`},bank:[`mustn't`,`don't have to`,`can't`,`shouldn't`],text:`You ___ bring food to the party; there will be plenty, but you can if you want.`,answers:[`don't have to`]},{id:`b2-wb-091`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Mustn't' means 'it is forbidden': You mustn't use your phone during the exam.`,wrongReasons:{"don't have to":`'don't have to' means 'it is not necessary', but leaving bags is forbidden, not optional.`,"needn't":`'needn't' means 'it is not necessary', which is too weak for a safety rule.`,"might not":`'might not' expresses possibility, not a rule.`},bank:[`mustn't`,`might not`,`don't have to`,`needn't`],text:`Passengers ___ leave their bags unattended anywhere in the airport.`,answers:[`mustn't`]},{id:`b2-wb-092`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Might have' + past participle means 'perhaps something happened': I can't find my keys; I might have left them at work.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must have missed' expresses certainty, but the speaker is not sure and offers other possibilities.`,should:`'should have missed' would suggest it was a good idea to miss the train, which makes no sense.`,"can't":`'can't have missed' means it is impossible, which contradicts 'I'm not sure'.`},bank:[`must`,`can't`,`might`,`should`],text:`I'm not sure where Tom is. He ___ have missed his train, or perhaps he's stuck in traffic.`,answers:[`might`]},{id:`b2-wb-093`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Needn't have' + past participle means you did something that was not necessary: You needn't have bought flowers.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't have cooked' is not a correct form for a past action that was unnecessary.`,"couldn't":`'couldn't have cooked' means it was impossible, but you clearly did cook.`,"wouldn't":`'wouldn't have cooked' is used in conditionals and does not express that cooking was unnecessary.`},bank:[`wouldn't`,`needn't`,`mustn't`,`couldn't`],text:`You ___ have cooked so much food; only four people came to dinner.`,answers:[`needn't`]},{id:`b2-wb-094`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Could' expresses general ability in the past: When I was young, I could swim for hours.`,wrongReasons:{can:`'can' is present; for ability in the past we use 'could'.`,must:`'must' expresses obligation or deduction, not past ability.`,should:`'should' gives advice, not ability in the past.`},bank:[`can`,`should`,`must`,`could`],text:`When she was only five, my sister ___ play the piano better than most adults.`,answers:[`could`]},{id:`b2-wb-095`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Was/were able to' is used for a specific success in the past: The fire spread, but everyone was able to escape.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must get out' expresses present obligation, not something the workers succeeded in doing in the past.`,can:`'can' is present, but the fire happened in the past.`,should:`'should' expresses advice, not what the workers succeeded in doing.`},bank:[`must`,`were able to`,`can`,`should`],text:`The building caught fire, but luckily all the workers ___ get out safely.`,answers:[`were able to`]},{id:`b2-wb-096`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Must' expresses a present deduction; 'can't' expresses that something is impossible: She must be tired. That can't be true.`,wrongReasons:{"0:can't":`'you can't be exhausted' would mean it is impossible, but twelve hours of work makes it very likely.`,"0:had to":`'had to' expresses past obligation; it cannot make a deduction about how you feel now.`,"0:may not":`'may not be' suggests the opposite of the logical conclusion after twelve hours of work.`,"1:must":`'That must be true' would mean we believe her, but the speaker is surprised and doubtful.`,"1:had to":`'That had to be true' is a past form and does not express the speaker's disbelief about what Anna says now.`,"1:may not":`'may not be true' is too weak for the speaker's strong disbelief.`},bank:[`may not`,`can't`,`had to`,`must`],text:`You've been working for twelve hours; you ___ be exhausted. But Anna says she isn't tired at all. That ___ be true!`,answers:[`must`,`can't`]},{id:`b2-wb-097`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Had better' + base verb gives strong advice: You'd better leave now or you'll miss the bus.`,wrongReasons:{"used to":`'used to book' describes a past habit, but the speaker is talking about what to do soon.`,ought:`'ought' needs 'to' before the verb (ought to book).`,"must have":`'must have book' is wrong; 'must have' is followed by a past participle and is used for deduction.`},bank:[`ought`,`used to`,`must have`,`had better`],text:`We ___ book the hotel soon; prices always go up in August.`,answers:[`had better`]},{id:`b2-wb-098`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'May' can express permission in a formal style: Visitors may take photos in the garden.`,wrongReasons:{must:`'must use' would mean guests are forced to swim, which is not the intended meaning.`,need:`'need use' is wrong; positive 'need' takes 'to' (need to use), and it means necessity.`,would:`'would use' describes a past habit or a hypothetical action, not permission.`},bank:[`may`,`would`,`must`,`need`],text:`Guests ___ use the swimming pool between 7 a.m. and 10 p.m.`,answers:[`may`]},{id:`b2-wb-099`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Couldn't have' + past participle means something was impossible in the past: He couldn't have stolen it; he was abroad.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'mustn't have' is not normally used for past deduction in British English; we use 'can't' or 'couldn't have'.`,"shouldn't":`'shouldn't have committed' expresses criticism, not impossibility.`,"needn't":`'needn't have committed' means it was unnecessary, which makes no sense here.`},bank:[`shouldn't`,`couldn't`,`needn't`,`mustn't`],text:`The suspect ___ have committed the crime because he was in hospital at the time.`,answers:[`couldn't`]},{id:`b2-wb-100`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`'Will' can express a confident prediction about the present: That'll be the postman at the door.`,wrongReasons:{can:`'That can be the plumber' expresses general possibility, not a confident guess about now.`,need:`'need' expresses necessity, not a prediction.`,ought:`'ought' needs 'to' before the verb.`},bank:[`need`,`ought`,`can`,`will`],text:`Someone is ringing the doorbell. That ___ be the plumber; he said he'd come at ten.`,answers:[`will`]},{id:`b2-wb-101`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"A lot more" intensifies a comparative.`,bank:[`many`,`lot`,`much`],text:`The film was a ___ more entertaining than I had expected.`,answers:[`lot`],wrongReasons:{many:`"Many" is used with countable nouns.`,much:`"Much more" does not take "a".`}},{id:`b2-wb-102`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Look for" means to search.`,bank:[`looking`,`searching`,`seeking`],text:`The company is ___ for a new marketing manager.`,answers:[`looking`],wrongReasons:{seeking:`"Seeking" needs no preposition.`,searching:`"Searching" needs "for" but cannot follow "is looking".`}},{id:`b2-wb-103`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"One of the + superlative plural" is the usual structure.`,bank:[`the`,`an`,`a`],text:`He's one of ___ most talented players I have ever coached.`,answers:[`the`],wrongReasons:{a:`"One of a most talented" is not grammatical.`,an:`"An" cannot be used before a plural noun.`}},{id:`b2-wb-104`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,bank:[`whom`,`whose`,`who`],text:`The man ___ car was stolen called the police.`,answers:[`whose`],wrongReasons:{who:`"Who" cannot show possession.`,whom:`"Whom" cannot show possession.`}},{id:`b2-wb-105`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Apply for" introduces the job.`,bank:[`to`,`at`,`for`],text:`He's applying ___ a job as a sales manager.`,answers:[`for`],wrongReasons:{to:`"Apply to" introduces the organisation.`,at:`"Apply at" introduces a place.`}},{id:`b2-wb-106`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"The sooner ... the earlier" is a double comparative.`,bank:[`earlier`,`early`,`earliest`],text:`The sooner we leave, the ___ we will arrive.`,answers:[`earlier`],wrongReasons:{early:`"Early" is the base form.`,earliest:`"Earliest" is a superlative.`}},{id:`b2-wb-107`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`Country names containing "United" take "the".`,bank:[`a`,`an`,`the`],text:`I've never been to ___ United States, but I would love to go.`,answers:[`the`],wrongReasons:{a:`"A" does not fit with this country name.`,an:`"An" does not fit with this country name.`}},{id:`b2-wb-108`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A "double room" has a bed for two.`,bank:[`double`,`doubled`,`doubly`],text:`I'd like to book a ___ room for two nights, please.`,answers:[`double`],wrongReasons:{doubled:`"Doubled" is a participle.`,doubly:`"Doubly" is an adverb.`}},{id:`b2-wb-109`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"The window" refers to a specific one both people can see.`,bank:[`a`,`the`,`an`],text:`Please close ___ window; it's freezing in here.`,answers:[`the`],wrongReasons:{a:`"A window" would not point to the specific one in the room.`,an:`"An" is used before vowel sounds only.`}},{id:`b2-wb-110`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Outgoing" means sociable.`,bank:[`outgoes`,`outgo`,`outgoing`],text:`She has a very ___ personality and gets on well with everyone.`,answers:[`outgoing`],wrongReasons:{outgo:`"Outgo" is rarely used.`,outgoes:`"Outgoes" is not a word.`}},{id:`b2-wb-111`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"One of those" selects a single item.`,bank:[`an`,`a`,`one`],text:`I'd like ___ of those cakes, please.`,answers:[`one`],wrongReasons:{a:`"A of those" is not grammatical.`,an:`"An of those" is not grammatical.`}},{id:`b2-wb-112`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Taken" is the past participle.`,bank:[`taken`,`take`,`took`],text:`I'm sorry, but all the seats are ___.`,answers:[`taken`],wrongReasons:{took:`A participle is needed.`,take:`"take" does not work here, because a participle is needed.`}},{id:`b2-wb-113`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Delayed" is the past participle.`,bank:[`delayed`,`delaying`,`delay`],text:`The flight to Madrid was ___ by two hours because of fog.`,answers:[`delayed`],wrongReasons:{delaying:`The passive needs a participle.`,delay:`"delay" does not work here, because the passive needs a participle.`}},{id:`b2-wb-114`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject.`,bank:[`whom`,`whose`,`who`],text:`I'd like to talk to the person ___ is in charge of the department.`,answers:[`who`],wrongReasons:{whom:`"Whom" is an object form.`,whose:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`b2-wb-115`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Take advantage of" is the fixed expression.`,bank:[`an advantage`,`advantage`,`the advantage`],text:`You should take ___ of the opportunity while you can.`,answers:[`advantage`],wrongReasons:{"the advantage":`"Take advantage of" has no article.`,"an advantage":`"An advantage" changes the meaning.`}},{id:`b2-wb-116`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Some" is used with uncountable nouns.`,bank:[`a`,`an`,`some`],text:`He gave me ___ advice about my health.`,answers:[`some`],wrongReasons:{an:`"Advice" is uncountable.`,a:`"a" does not work here, because "Advice" is uncountable.`}},{id:`b2-wb-117`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Herself" is the reflexive pronoun.`,bank:[`hers`,`herself`,`her`],text:`She cut ___ with a kitchen knife.`,answers:[`herself`],wrongReasons:{her:`"Her" refers to another person.`,hers:`"Hers" is a possessive pronoun.`}},{id:`b2-wb-118`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Arrested for" introduces the offence.`,bank:[`for`,`of`,`with`],text:`They were arrested ___ drunk driving.`,answers:[`for`],wrongReasons:{of:`"Arrested of" is not correct.`,with:`"Arrested with" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-119`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A "journey" is a trip from one place to another.`,bank:[`trip`,`journey`,`travel`],text:`The ___ from London to Edinburgh takes about four and a half hours by train.`,answers:[`journey`],wrongReasons:{travel:`"Travel" is uncountable.`,trip:`A "trip" is a short journey.`}},{id:`b2-wb-120`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Which" chooses from a limited set.`,bank:[`who`,`which`,`what`],text:`He asked me ___ of the two dresses I preferred.`,answers:[`which`],wrongReasons:{what:`"What" does not choose from two.`,who:`"Who" refers to people.`}},{id:`b2-wb-121`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"When" introduces a time clause.`,bank:[`while`,`during`,`when`],text:`I'll call you ___ I arrive at the station.`,answers:[`when`],wrongReasons:{during:`"During" is followed by a noun.`,while:`"While" is used with longer actions.`}},{id:`b2-wb-122`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Look for" means to search.`,bank:[`for`,`at`,`after`],text:`The police are looking ___ the missing documents.`,answers:[`for`],wrongReasons:{at:`"Look at" means to direct your eyes.`,after:`"Look after" means to take care of.`}},{id:`b2-wb-123`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"A" introduces a singular countable noun.`,bank:[`an`,`a`,`the`],text:`I'd like to ask you ___ question about the exam.`,answers:[`a`],wrongReasons:{the:`A specific question has not been mentioned.`,an:`"Question" starts with a consonant sound.`}},{id:`b2-wb-124`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Couldn't have been more pleased" means extremely pleased.`,bank:[`most`,`more`,`much`],text:`He couldn't have been ___ pleased with the result.`,answers:[`more`],wrongReasons:{most:`"Most" cannot be followed by "than" here.`,much:`"Much pleased" is formal.`}},{id:`b2-wb-125`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"The number of" takes a singular verb.`,bank:[`have`,`has`,`is`],text:`The number of homeless people ___ risen sharply.`,answers:[`has`],wrongReasons:{have:`"The number" is singular.`,is:`"Is risen" is not a correct perfect.`}},{id:`b2-wb-126`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"What" introduces a general question.`,bank:[`what`,`who`,`which`],text:`He asked me ___ I wanted to eat.`,answers:[`what`],wrongReasons:{which:`"Which" would need a limited choice.`,who:`"Who" refers to people.`}},{id:`b2-wb-127`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Under pressure" is the fixed collocation.`,bank:[`been under`,`been at`,`been in`],text:`The team has ___ a lot of pressure since the new CEO arrived.`,answers:[`been under`],wrongReasons:{"been in":`"In pressure" is not a standard collocation.`,"been at":`"At pressure" is not a standard collocation.`}},{id:`b2-wb-128`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Can't understand" uses the base form.`,bank:[`understanding`,`understand`,`understood`],text:`I can't ___ why he behaved so rudely.`,answers:[`understand`],wrongReasons:{understood:`"Can't" is followed by the base form.`,understanding:`"understanding" does not work here, because "Can't" is followed by the base form.`}},{id:`b2-wb-129`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A "shelter" is a place of refuge.`,bank:[`shell`,`shelf`,`shelter`],text:`She volunteers at a local ___ for the homeless.`,answers:[`shelter`],wrongReasons:{shelf:`A "shelf" holds objects.`,shell:`A "shell" is a hard cover.`}},{id:`b2-wb-130`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Any" is used in negative sentences.`,bank:[`many`,`a few`,`any`],text:`We didn't have ___ trouble finding the house.`,answers:[`any`],wrongReasons:{many:`"Trouble" is uncountable.`,"a few":`"a few" does not work here, because "Trouble" is uncountable.`}},{id:`b2-wb-131`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"The most suitable" is the superlative of a long adjective.`,bank:[`most`,`more`,`much`],text:`Of all the candidates, Maria seemed the ___ suitable.`,answers:[`most`],wrongReasons:{more:`With several candidates the superlative is needed.`,much:`"Much" cannot form a superlative.`}},{id:`b2-wb-132`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Has employed" is the present perfect.`,bank:[`employ`,`employed`,`employing`],text:`The company has ___ over three hundred people this year.`,answers:[`employed`],wrongReasons:{employing:`An auxiliary needs a participle.`,employ:`"employ" does not work here, because an auxiliary needs a participle.`}},{id:`b2-wb-133`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Arrive at" is used with smaller places.`,bank:[`on`,`at`,`in`],text:`We arrived ___ the hotel at midnight.`,answers:[`at`],wrongReasons:{in:`"In" is used with cities and countries.`,on:`"On" is used with surfaces.`}},{id:`b2-wb-134`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Give a presentation" is the standard collocation.`,bank:[`make`,`give`,`do`],text:`I have to ___ a presentation to the board on Monday.`,answers:[`give`],wrongReasons:{do:`"Do a presentation" is less standard than "give".`,make:`"Make" is used with speeches or decisions.`}},{id:`b2-wb-135`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Make it up" means to reconcile.`,bank:[`did`,`made`,`got`],text:`They had a quarrel, but they ___ it up quickly.`,answers:[`made`],wrongReasons:{did:`"Do it up" has a different meaning.`,got:`"Get it up" is not an idiom.`}},{id:`b2-wb-136`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Look on the bright side" means to be positive.`,bank:[`high`,`bright`,`light`],text:`He is the sort of person who always looks on the ___ side of things.`,answers:[`bright`],wrongReasons:{light:`"Light side" is not the idiom.`,high:`"High side" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`b2-wb-137`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Left" is the past simple.`,bank:[`leaving`,`left`,`leave`],text:`We ___ for the airport three hours before our flight.`,answers:[`left`],wrongReasons:{leaving:`An auxiliary is missing.`,leave:`A past form is needed.`}},{id:`b2-wb-138`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Furniture" is uncountable.`,bank:[`furnitures`,`furniture`,`a furniture`],text:`She bought a lot of ___ for her new flat.`,answers:[`furniture`],wrongReasons:{furnitures:`"Furniture" is uncountable.`,"a furniture":`"a furniture" does not work here, because "Furniture" is uncountable.`}},{id:`b2-wb-139`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Any" is used in negative sentences.`,bank:[`any`,`many`,`few`],text:`We haven't got ___ tickets left; the show is sold out.`,answers:[`any`],wrongReasons:{many:`"Many" is used in negative sentences too, but "any" is the neutral choice.`,few:`"Few" is not used with a negative verb.`}},{id:`b2-wb-140`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"The reason why" is the usual structure.`,bank:[`what`,`why`,`who`],text:`That is the reason ___ I left.`,answers:[`why`],wrongReasons:{what:`"What" cannot follow "reason".`,who:`"Who" refers to people.`}},{id:`b2-wb-141`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Remarkably" modifies the adjective "talented".`,bank:[`remarking`,`remarkable`,`remarkably`],text:`She is a ___ talented musician who plays three instruments.`,answers:[`remarkably`],wrongReasons:{remarkable:`An adverb is needed before the adjective "talented".`,remarking:`"Remarking" is a participle.`}},{id:`b2-wb-142`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"On holiday" is the fixed expression.`,bank:[`in`,`at`,`on`],text:`They are going ___ holiday to Spain next week.`,answers:[`on`],wrongReasons:{in:`"In holiday" is not standard.`,at:`"At holiday" is not standard.`}},{id:`b2-wb-143`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Launch" means to introduce a new product to the market.`,bank:[`launched`,`lunched`,`lurched`],text:`The company ___ a new product last week, and it is already selling well.`,answers:[`launched`],wrongReasons:{lunched:`"Lunched" means had a midday meal.`,lurched:`"Lurched" means moved unsteadily.`}},{id:`b2-wb-144`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Rely on" is the fixed collocation.`,bank:[`on`,`at`,`in`],text:`Many people rely ___ the internet for news.`,answers:[`on`],wrongReasons:{in:`"Rely in" is not correct.`,at:`"Rely at" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-145`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Turn off" is the phrasal verb.`,bank:[`take`,`turn`,`put`],text:`Make sure you ___ the lights off before you go to bed.`,answers:[`turn`],wrongReasons:{put:`"Put the lights off" is not standard.`,take:`"Take the lights off" is not standard.`}},{id:`b2-wb-146`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Famous for" gives the reason for fame.`,bank:[`with`,`for`,`of`],text:`The company is famous ___ its excellent customer service.`,answers:[`for`],wrongReasons:{of:`"Famous of" is not standard.`,with:`"Famous with" is not standard.`}},{id:`b2-wb-147`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Die of" an illness is the standard collocation.`,bank:[`of`,`with`,`from`],text:`The old man died ___ cancer last year.`,answers:[`of`],wrongReasons:{from:`"Died from" is also used, but "of" is the usual collocation for illness.`,with:`"Died with" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`b2-wb-148`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"On" is used with floors.`,bank:[`on`,`in`,`at`],text:`He lives in a flat ___ the third floor.`,answers:[`on`],wrongReasons:{in:`"In the third floor" is not the standard form.`,at:`"At the third floor" is not the standard form.`}},{id:`b2-wb-149`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"A" introduces a new singular noun.`,bank:[`the`,`an`,`a`],text:`There is ___ new restaurant on our street.`,answers:[`a`],wrongReasons:{the:`"The" would assume that the listener knows which restaurant.`,an:`"An" is used before vowel sounds only.`}},{id:`b2-wb-150`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Shouldn't have" criticises a past action.`,bank:[`shouldn't`,`can't`,`needn't`],text:`It was a mistake. You ___ have trusted him.`,answers:[`shouldn't`],wrongReasons:{"needn't":`"Needn't have" means it was unnecessary.`,"can't":`"Can't have" is a deduction.`}},{id:`b2-wb-151`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Had better" gives strong advice.`,bank:[`would better`,`had better`,`should better`],text:`If you want to pass, you ___ really start studying.`,answers:[`had better`],wrongReasons:{"would better":`"Would better" is not used.`,"should better":`"Should better" is not used.`}},{id:`b2-wb-152`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"On the corner" is the usual collocation.`,bank:[`at`,`in`,`on`],text:`The shop is ___ the corner of the street.`,answers:[`on`],wrongReasons:{in:`"In the corner" refers to the inside of a room.`,at:`"At the corner" is also used but "on the corner" is the usual collocation.`}},{id:`b2-wb-153`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Ought to have" + participle expresses criticism.`,bank:[`must`,`should`,`ought`],text:`You ___ to have asked me before you borrowed it.`,answers:[`ought`],wrongReasons:{should:`"Should" would not take "to have" here.`,must:`"Must" would not take "to have" here.`}},{id:`b2-wb-154`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Can well imagine" expresses ability to understand.`,bank:[`should`,`can`,`must`],text:`I ___ well imagine how difficult it was for her.`,answers:[`can`],wrongReasons:{must:`"Must imagine" has a different meaning.`,should:`"Should imagine" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`b2-wb-155`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A "queue" is a line of people.`,bank:[`quest`,`queen`,`queue`],text:`There was a long ___ at the check-in desk.`,answers:[`queue`],wrongReasons:{quest:`"Quest" is a long search.`,queen:`"Queen" is a female ruler.`}},{id:`b2-wb-156`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Helpful" is the adjective that describes a person who gives assistance.`,bank:[`helpfully`,`help`,`helpful`],text:`The shop assistant was very ___ and helped us to find exactly what we needed.`,answers:[`helpful`],wrongReasons:{help:`"Help" is a verb or noun, but an adjective is needed after "was very".`,helpfully:`"Helpfully" is an adverb and cannot follow "was very".`}},{id:`b2-wb-157`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Very little" emphasises how small the amount is.`,bank:[`very`,`so`,`a`],text:`There is ___ little hope of finding the missing child.`,answers:[`very`],wrongReasons:{a:`"A little" would be positive.`,so:`"So little" needs a result clause.`}},{id:`b2-wb-158`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Enough" follows the adjective.`,bank:[`enough`,`too`,`very`],text:`I'm afraid your work is not good ___ to publish.`,answers:[`enough`],wrongReasons:{too:`"Too good to publish" changes the meaning.`,very:`"Very good to publish" is not natural.`}},{id:`b2-wb-159`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"A pair of shoes" is the standard expression.`,bank:[`one`,`a couple`,`a pair`],text:`I'd like to buy ___ of those red shoes in the window.`,answers:[`a pair`],wrongReasons:{"a couple":`"A couple" needs "of".`,one:`"One of those shoes" would refer to a single shoe.`}},{id:`b2-wb-160`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Whatever you do" means no matter what you do.`,bank:[`Whichever`,`Whatever`,`However`],text:`___ you do, don't forget to lock the door.`,answers:[`Whatever`],wrongReasons:{Whichever:`"Whichever" refers to a limited choice.`,However:`"However" shows manner.`}},{id:`b2-wb-161`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Long-standing" means existing for a long time.`,bank:[`long-stand`,`long-stood`,`long-standing`],text:`Our company has a ___ policy of recruiting graduates.`,answers:[`long-standing`],wrongReasons:{"long-stood":`This compound is not standard.`,"long-stand":`"long-stand" does not work here, because this compound is not standard.`}},{id:`b2-wb-162`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"On the corner" is the usual collocation.`,bank:[`on`,`in`,`at`],text:`The shop is ___ the corner of the high street.`,answers:[`on`],wrongReasons:{in:`"In the corner" refers to a room.`,at:`"At the corner" is also used but "on" is standard for a street corner.`}},{id:`b2-wb-163`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Plenty of" means a large amount.`,bank:[`much`,`many`,`plenty`],text:`There was ___ of food at the party; we couldn't eat it all.`,answers:[`plenty`],wrongReasons:{many:`"Many of food" is not grammatical.`,much:`"Much of food" is not natural.`}},{id:`b2-wb-164`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Took off" is the past simple.`,bank:[`take`,`took`,`taken`],text:`The plane ___ off an hour late.`,answers:[`took`],wrongReasons:{take:`The sentence is in the past.`,taken:`An auxiliary is missing.`}},{id:`b2-wb-165`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A "queue" is a line of people waiting.`,bank:[`quest`,`queue`,`quote`],text:`There was a long ___ outside the cinema, so we decided to see another film.`,answers:[`queue`],wrongReasons:{quest:`A "quest" is a long search for something.`,quote:`A "quote" is a repeated passage or a price estimate.`}},{id:`b2-wb-166`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Difficult" is the adjective.`,bank:[`difficult`,`difficultly`,`difficulty`],text:`The question was so ___ that nobody could answer it.`,answers:[`difficult`],wrongReasons:{difficulty:`"Difficulty" is a noun.`,difficultly:`"Difficultly" is not a word.`}},{id:`b2-wb-167`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"In a calm voice" is the standard phrase.`,bank:[`in`,`by`,`with`],text:`She spoke ___ a calm voice, though she was upset.`,answers:[`in`],wrongReasons:{with:`"With a calm voice" is possible but "in" is the usual collocation.`,by:`"By a calm voice" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-168`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`An "approach" is a way of dealing with a problem.`,bank:[`approach`,`approaching`,`approached`],text:`Her ___ to the problem was original and surprised everybody.`,answers:[`approach`],wrongReasons:{approaching:`"Approaching" is a participle and cannot follow "her" as a noun.`,approached:`"Approached" is a past participle, not a noun.`}},{id:`b2-wb-169`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Tackle" means to deal with a problem.`,bank:[`tack`,`tickle`,`tackle`],text:`The government has promised to ___ the problem of homelessness.`,answers:[`tackle`],wrongReasons:{tickle:`"Tickle" means to touch lightly.`,tack:`"Tack" is a small nail.`}},{id:`b2-wb-170`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Family-run" is a compound adjective.`,bank:[`family-run`,`family-running`,`family-ran`],text:`The travel agent recommended a ___ hotel near the beach.`,answers:[`family-run`],wrongReasons:{"family-running":`This compound is not standard.`,"family-ran":`"family-ran" does not work here, because this compound is not standard.`}},{id:`b2-wb-171`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Likely to" expresses probability.`,bank:[`like`,`liked`,`likely`],text:`The weather is ___ to change before the weekend.`,answers:[`likely`],wrongReasons:{like:`"Like" is a preposition.`,liked:`"Liked" is a participle.`}},{id:`b2-wb-172`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"May not" can express a formal prohibition.`,bank:[`can't`,`may`,`need`],text:`You ___ not smoke on the premises.`,answers:[`may`],wrongReasons:{"can't":`"Can't not" is a double negative.`,need:`"Need not smoke" would mean it is not necessary.`}},{id:`b2-wb-173`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Puzzled" describes his feeling.`,bank:[`puzzle`,`puzzling`,`puzzled`],text:`He gave me a ___ look when I told him the news.`,answers:[`puzzled`],wrongReasons:{puzzling:`"Puzzling" describes the cause.`,puzzle:`"Puzzle" is a noun or verb.`}},{id:`b2-wb-174`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Not nearly as ... as" means far from.`,bank:[`nearly`,`near`,`nearby`],text:`The film was not ___ as good as the book.`,answers:[`nearly`],wrongReasons:{near:`"Near" is an adjective or preposition.`,nearby:`"Nearby" means close in place.`}},{id:`b2-wb-175`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject of "was responsible".`,bank:[`whose`,`whom`,`who`],text:`He is the man ___ I think was responsible for the accident.`,answers:[`who`],wrongReasons:{whom:`"Whom" is an object form but here it is the subject of "was".`,whose:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`b2-wb-176`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Let" fits the structure "let it get too hot".`,bank:[`had`,`let`,`made`],text:`He ___ the pan too hot and burnt the onions.`,answers:[`let`],wrongReasons:{made:`"Made the pan too hot" has a different structure.`,had:`"Had the pan too hot" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`b2-wb-177`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"A narrow escape" means a close call.`,bank:[`narrowly`,`narrowness`,`narrow`],text:`She had a ___ escape when the car skidded off the road.`,answers:[`narrow`],wrongReasons:{narrowly:`"Narrowly" is an adverb.`,narrowness:`"Narrowness" is a noun.`}},{id:`b2-wb-178`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Be respected" is the passive.`,bank:[`respect`,`respected`,`respecting`],text:`He was ___ by his colleagues for his honesty and hard work.`,answers:[`respected`],wrongReasons:{respecting:`"Respecting" is active, but he is the receiver.`,respect:`"Respect" is a noun or base verb.`}},{id:`b2-wb-179`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Has introduced" is the present perfect.`,bank:[`introducing`,`introduced`,`introduce`],text:`The firm has ___ a new policy on working from home.`,answers:[`introduced`],wrongReasons:{introducing:`An auxiliary needs a participle.`,introduce:`"introduce" does not work here, because an auxiliary needs a participle.`}},{id:`b2-wb-180`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Since" introduces a starting point.`,bank:[`since`,`from`,`for`],text:`He's been in a bad mood ___ he lost his job.`,answers:[`since`],wrongReasons:{for:`"For" is used with a length of time.`,from:`"From" does not fit the present perfect.`}},{id:`b2-wb-181`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Wherever you like" means in any place you choose.`,bank:[`somewhere`,`wherever`,`everywhere`],text:`You can park ___ you like; the car park is empty.`,answers:[`wherever`],wrongReasons:{everywhere:`"Everywhere" does not express a free choice.`,somewhere:`"Somewhere" is not followed by "you like".`}},{id:`b2-wb-182`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"An" is used before a vowel sound.`,bank:[`an`,`the`,`a`],text:`She's ___ honest person, so you can trust her completely.`,answers:[`an`],wrongReasons:{a:`The "h" in "honest" is silent, so the word begins with a vowel sound.`,the:`"The" would refer to a specific person already mentioned.`}},{id:`b2-wb-183`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Postponed" is the past participle.`,bank:[`postpone`,`postponed`,`postponing`],text:`The meeting was ___ until next week because the director was ill.`,answers:[`postponed`],wrongReasons:{postponing:`The passive needs a participle.`,postpone:`"postpone" does not work here, because the passive needs a participle.`}},{id:`b2-wb-184`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Submit" means to hand in formally.`,bank:[`admit`,`submit`,`submit up`],text:`The students must ___ their assignments by Friday.`,answers:[`submit`],wrongReasons:{"submit up":`"Submit up" is not a phrasal verb.`,admit:`"Admit" means to confess.`}},{id:`b2-wb-185`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Since" is used with a starting point.`,bank:[`for`,`since`,`from`],text:`She has lived here ___ 2010.`,answers:[`since`],wrongReasons:{for:`"For" is used with a length of time.`,from:`"From" does not combine with the present perfect.`}},{id:`b2-wb-186`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Better" is the comparative.`,bank:[`best`,`better`,`good`],text:`She plays the piano ___ than her sister.`,answers:[`better`],wrongReasons:{best:`"Best" is a superlative.`,good:`"Good" is the base form.`}},{id:`b2-wb-187`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`The present simple describes a regular timetable.`,bank:[`leaves`,`leave`,`leaving`],text:`The bus to the airport ___ every twenty minutes from the main station.`,answers:[`leaves`],wrongReasons:{leaving:`An auxiliary verb is missing.`,leave:`A third-person singular subject needs "-s".`}},{id:`b2-wb-188`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Grateful to someone for something".`,bank:[`for`,`of`,`to`],text:`I'm grateful ___ you for all your help.`,answers:[`to`],wrongReasons:{of:`"Grateful of" is not correct.`,for:`"Grateful for" introduces the thing, not the person.`}},{id:`b2-wb-189`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"By far" strengthens a superlative.`,bank:[`by far`,`for far`,`far by`],text:`He is ___ the best player in the team, without any doubt.`,answers:[`by far`],wrongReasons:{"far by":`The word order is wrong.`,"for far":`"For far" is not a collocation.`}},{id:`b2-wb-190`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Fed up with" is the standard collocation.`,bank:[`of`,`with`,`from`],text:`I'm fed up ___ his constant complaints.`,answers:[`with`],wrongReasons:{of:`"Fed up of" is informal.`,from:`"Fed up from" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-191`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Out of" means with none left.`,bank:[`away`,`off`,`out`],text:`We are ___ of milk; could you buy some?`,answers:[`out`],wrongReasons:{off:`"Off of" is informal and wrong here.`,away:`"Away of" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-192`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Do a favour" is the standard collocation.`,bank:[`give`,`do`,`make`],text:`He asked me to ___ him a favour.`,answers:[`do`],wrongReasons:{make:`"Make a favour" is not a collocation.`,give:`"Give a favour" is not a collocation.`}},{id:`b2-wb-193`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"None of us" means no one in the group.`,bank:[`neither`,`none`,`no`],text:`The film was so boring that ___ of us stayed to the end.`,answers:[`none`],wrongReasons:{neither:`"Neither" is only used for two people.`,no:`"No of us" is not grammatical.`}},{id:`b2-wb-194`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Their" is the possessive adjective used with a noun.`,bank:[`there`,`their`,`they're`],text:`The teacher gave each of the students ___ own task.`,answers:[`their`],wrongReasons:{there:`"There" is a place adverb.`,"they're":`"They're" means "they are".`}},{id:`b2-wb-195`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Discuss" is a verb.`,bank:[`discuss`,`discussing`,`discussion`],text:`The students were asked to ___ their ideas in groups.`,answers:[`discuss`],wrongReasons:{discussion:`"Discussion" is a noun.`,discussing:`After "to" use the base form.`}},{id:`b2-wb-196`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Switch off" means to turn off.`,bank:[`switch out`,`switch off`,`switch over`],text:`The teacher asked the students to ___ their mobile phones.`,answers:[`switch off`],wrongReasons:{"switch out":`"Switch out" is not a phrasal verb in this sense.`,"switch over":`"Switch over" means to change channels.`}},{id:`b2-wb-197`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Suspected of" introduces the alleged crime.`,bank:[`for`,`with`,`of`],text:`He is suspected ___ taking part in the robbery.`,answers:[`of`],wrongReasons:{for:`"Suspected for" is not correct.`,with:`"Suspected with" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-198`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Be known for" introduces a reputation.`,bank:[`knowledge`,`known`,`knowing`],text:`He is ___ for his generosity and kindness.`,answers:[`known`],wrongReasons:{knowing:`"Knowing" is a participle.`,knowledge:`"Knowledge" is a noun.`}},{id:`b2-wb-199`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Each" is followed by a singular noun.`,bank:[`each`,`all`,`both`],text:`The teacher spoke to ___ student separately about their progress.`,answers:[`each`],wrongReasons:{all:`"All" needs a plural noun.`,both:`"Both" refers to two people.`}},{id:`b2-wb-200`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Has risen" is the present perfect.`,bank:[`raised`,`risen`,`rose`],text:`The cost of food has ___ sharply in the last six months.`,answers:[`risen`],wrongReasons:{rose:`A participle is needed after "has".`,raised:`"Raise" needs an object.`}},{id:`b2-wb-201`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Interested in" is the fixed collocation.`,bank:[`in`,`at`,`on`],text:`I'm not interested ___ buying an expensive car.`,answers:[`in`],wrongReasons:{on:`"Interested on" is not correct.`,at:`"Interested at" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-202`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Turn down" means to refuse.`,bank:[`take`,`turn`,`put`],text:`She was offered a job but decided to ___ it down.`,answers:[`turn`],wrongReasons:{put:`"Put down" means to place.`,take:`"Take down" means to remove.`}},{id:`b2-wb-203`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Can't have" is a negative deduction about the past.`,bank:[`should`,`must`,`can't`],text:`She ___ have heard the alarm; she was fast asleep.`,answers:[`can't`],wrongReasons:{must:`"Must have" would contradict "fast asleep".`,should:`"Should have" expresses expectation.`}},{id:`b2-wb-204`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,bank:[`who`,`whom`,`whose`],text:`The man ___ car was stolen is very upset.`,answers:[`whose`],wrongReasons:{who:`"Who" cannot show possession.`,whom:`"Whom" cannot show possession.`}},{id:`b2-wb-205`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Some" is used with uncountable nouns in requests.`,bank:[`a`,`some`,`an`],text:`I'd like to buy ___ bread, please; I don't need a whole loaf.`,answers:[`some`],wrongReasons:{a:`"Bread" is uncountable and cannot take "a".`,an:`"Bread" is uncountable and cannot take "an".`}},{id:`b2-wb-206`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"I would rather you told" expresses preference.`,bank:[`would`,`should`,`had`],text:`I ___ rather you told me the truth than lied to me.`,answers:[`would`],wrongReasons:{had:`"Had rather" is old-fashioned.`,should:`"Should rather" is not used.`}},{id:`b2-wb-207`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Since" introduces a starting point.`,bank:[`from`,`for`,`since`],text:`He has been working in this department ___ 2015.`,answers:[`since`],wrongReasons:{for:`"For" needs a length of time.`,from:`"From" does not combine with the present perfect.`}},{id:`b2-wb-208`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Prefer X to Y" uses "to" between the two things.`,bank:[`than`,`to`,`over`],text:`She prefers tea ___ coffee, especially in the morning.`,answers:[`to`],wrongReasons:{than:`"Prefer than" is not correct.`,over:`"Prefer over" is not standard.`}},{id:`b2-wb-209`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Difficult" is the adjective before a noun.`,bank:[`difficultly`,`difficult`,`difficulty`],text:`It was a ___ decision, and nobody was happy about it.`,answers:[`difficult`],wrongReasons:{difficulty:`"Difficulty" is a noun and cannot describe a decision.`,difficultly:`"Difficultly" is not a word.`}},{id:`b2-wb-210`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Bound to" expresses near-certainty.`,bank:[`bound`,`supposed up`,`sure up`],text:`He is ___ to arrive late; he always does.`,answers:[`bound`],wrongReasons:{"sure up":`"Sure up" is not correct.`,"supposed up":`"Supposed up" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-211`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"So + adjective + that" shows a result.`,bank:[`too`,`so`,`such`],text:`The speaker was ___ boring that half the audience left.`,answers:[`so`],wrongReasons:{such:`"Such" needs a noun phrase.`,too:`"Too" does not take "that".`}},{id:`b2-wb-212`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A "gap year" is a year between school and university.`,bank:[`gasp`,`gape`,`gap`],text:`Many students take a ___ year before starting university.`,answers:[`gap`],wrongReasons:{gape:`"Gape" means to stare.`,gasp:`"Gasp" means to breathe in suddenly.`}},{id:`b2-wb-213`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"So + adjective + that" shows a result.`,bank:[`too`,`so`,`such`],text:`The weather was ___ cold that we stayed indoors all day.`,answers:[`so`],wrongReasons:{such:`"Such" needs a noun phrase.`,too:`"Too" does not take "that".`}},{id:`b2-wb-214`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Must have entered" is a strong deduction.`,bank:[`needn't`,`can't`,`must`],text:`The thieves ___ have entered through the window; the door was locked.`,answers:[`must`],wrongReasons:{"can't":`"Can't have" would contradict the evidence.`,"needn't":`"Needn't have" means it was unnecessary.`}},{id:`b2-wb-215`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Several" goes with countable plural nouns.`,bank:[`much`,`several`,`a little`],text:`There are ___ interesting books in the library about local history.`,answers:[`several`],wrongReasons:{much:`"Books" is countable and plural.`,"a little":`"A little" is used with uncountable nouns.`}},{id:`b2-wb-216`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Couldn't" expresses past inability.`,bank:[`mustn't`,`couldn't`,`needn't`],text:`We ___ get tickets, so we watched the match on TV.`,answers:[`couldn't`],wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`"Mustn't" is a prohibition.`,"needn't":`"Needn't" is about necessity.`}},{id:`b2-wb-217`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Communication skills" is a standard collocation.`,bank:[`communicate`,`communicating`,`communication`],text:`The company is looking for someone with excellent ___ skills.`,answers:[`communication`],wrongReasons:{communicate:`"Communicate" is a verb.`,communicating:`"Communicating" is a participle.`}},{id:`b2-wb-218`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"So + adjective + that" shows a result.`,bank:[`such`,`so`,`too`],text:`The children were ___ excited about the trip that they couldn't sleep.`,answers:[`so`],wrongReasons:{such:`"Such" needs a noun phrase.`,too:`"Too" does not introduce a result with "that".`}},{id:`b2-wb-219`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Considerably" intensifies a comparative.`,bank:[`considerably`,`considerable`,`considering`],text:`Prices in the capital are ___ higher than in the countryside.`,answers:[`considerably`],wrongReasons:{considerable:`An adverb is needed to modify a comparative.`,considering:`"Considering" is a participle and cannot modify a comparative.`}},{id:`b2-wb-220`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Lovely" is an adjective.`,bank:[`love`,`loved`,`lovely`],text:`It was ___ to see you at the party after so many years.`,answers:[`lovely`],wrongReasons:{love:`"Love" is a noun or verb.`,loved:`"Loved" is a participle.`}},{id:`b2-wb-221`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Rely on" is the fixed collocation.`,bank:[`on`,`at`,`in`],text:`We rely ___ public transport to get to work.`,answers:[`on`],wrongReasons:{at:`"Rely at" is not correct.`,in:`"Rely in" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-222`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Made from" is used when the material has been changed.`,bank:[`of`,`with`,`from`],text:`Paper is made ___ wood.`,answers:[`from`],wrongReasons:{of:`"Made of" is used when the material is still recognisable.`,with:`"Made with" is not standard here.`}},{id:`b2-wb-223`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"An" is used before a vowel sound.`,bank:[`the`,`an`,`a`],text:`She has ___ interesting job in the music industry.`,answers:[`an`],wrongReasons:{a:`"Interesting" starts with a vowel sound.`,the:`A specific job has not been mentioned.`}},{id:`b2-wb-224`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Little progress" means almost none.`,bank:[`few`,`a few`,`little`],text:`She has made ___ progress this term.`,answers:[`little`],wrongReasons:{few:`"Progress" is uncountable.`,"a few":`"a few" does not work here, because "Progress" is uncountable.`}},{id:`b2-wb-225`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Due to land" uses the base form.`,bank:[`lands`,`land`,`landing`],text:`The plane is due to ___ at 8.30.`,answers:[`land`],wrongReasons:{landing:`After "to" use the base form.`,lands:`"lands" does not work here, because after "to" use the base form.`}},{id:`b2-wb-226`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Raise awareness" is a standard collocation.`,bank:[`awareful`,`awareness`,`aware`],text:`The festival aims to raise ___ of the dangers of climate change.`,answers:[`awareness`],wrongReasons:{aware:`"Aware" is an adjective.`,awareful:`This word does not exist.`}},{id:`b2-wb-227`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Working conditions" is the standard collocation.`,bank:[`conditions`,`conditioning`,`condition`],text:`The workers are demanding better working ___.`,answers:[`conditions`],wrongReasons:{condition:`The collocation uses the plural.`,conditioning:`"Conditioning" means training.`}},{id:`b2-wb-228`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`A "cottage" is a small house in the country.`,bank:[`cottager`,`cottage`,`cottaging`],text:`We're staying in a lovely ___ with a view of the harbour.`,answers:[`cottage`],wrongReasons:{cottager:`A "cottager" is a person.`,cottaging:`"Cottaging" is a slang word.`}},{id:`b2-wb-229`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Show" means to present for inspection.`,bank:[`show`,`reveal`,`prove`],text:`You have to ___ your boarding pass at the gate.`,answers:[`show`],wrongReasons:{prove:`"Prove" means to demonstrate the truth.`,reveal:`"Reveal" means to make known.`}},{id:`b2-wb-230`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Go on a cruise" is the fixed collocation.`,bank:[`in`,`at`,`on`],text:`They are going ___ a cruise next month.`,answers:[`on`],wrongReasons:{in:`"In a cruise" is not a standard expression.`,at:`"At a cruise" is not a standard expression.`}},{id:`b2-wb-231`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,bank:[`whom`,`who`,`whose`],text:`Have you met the woman ___ car broke down outside our house?`,answers:[`whose`],wrongReasons:{who:`"Who" cannot show possession.`,whom:`"Whom" cannot show possession.`}},{id:`b2-wb-232`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Get lost" is a standard expression.`,bank:[`made`,`had`,`got`],text:`The tourists ___ lost in the narrow streets of the old town.`,answers:[`got`],wrongReasons:{had:`"Had lost" would change the structure.`,made:`"Made lost" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-233`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Pointed out" is the past simple.`,bank:[`pointing`,`point`,`pointed`],text:`The tour guide ___ out the main sights as we drove through the city.`,answers:[`pointed`],wrongReasons:{pointing:`An auxiliary is missing.`,point:`A past form is needed.`}},{id:`b2-wb-234`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Apologise for" introduces the reason.`,bank:[`of`,`to`,`for`],text:`He apologised ___ the delay.`,answers:[`for`],wrongReasons:{of:`"Apologise of" is not correct.`,to:`"Apologise to" introduces the person.`}},{id:`b2-wb-235`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Far" intensifies a comparative.`,bank:[`too`,`very`,`far`],text:`The film was ___ more exciting than the book, which I found rather slow.`,answers:[`far`],wrongReasons:{very:`"Very" cannot modify a comparative.`,too:`"Too" does not fit in a comparison.`}},{id:`b2-wb-236`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Better" is the comparative.`,bank:[`better`,`best`,`good`],text:`The meal was ___ than I had expected.`,answers:[`better`],wrongReasons:{best:`"Best" is a superlative.`,good:`"Good" is the base form.`}},{id:`b2-wb-237`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Addicted to" is the fixed collocation.`,bank:[`of`,`with`,`to`],text:`She is addicted ___ chocolate and eats it every day.`,answers:[`to`],wrongReasons:{of:`"Addicted of" is not correct.`,with:`"Addicted with" is not correct.`}},{id:`b2-wb-238`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Distinguish between" means to see the difference.`,bank:[`distinguish`,`distinction`,`distinct`],text:`It is important to ___ between facts and opinions when you read the news.`,answers:[`distinguish`],wrongReasons:{distinct:`"Distinct" is an adjective and cannot follow "to".`,distinction:`"Distinction" is a noun and cannot follow "to".`}},{id:`b2-wb-239`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Under the impression" means believing.`,bank:[`in`,`under`,`on`],text:`They were ___ the impression that the shop was closed.`,answers:[`under`],wrongReasons:{in:`"In the impression" is not standard.`,on:`"On the impression" is not standard.`}},{id:`b2-wb-240`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Any" is used in negative sentences.`,bank:[`no`,`some`,`any`],text:`He didn't give me ___ explanation for his strange behaviour.`,answers:[`any`],wrongReasons:{no:`"Didn't give no" is a double negative.`,some:`"Some" is not used in negatives like this.`}},{id:`b2-wb-241`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Go on a picnic" is the fixed expression.`,bank:[`in`,`on`,`at`],text:`We're going ___ a picnic if the weather is good.`,answers:[`on`],wrongReasons:{in:`"In a picnic" is not standard.`,at:`"At a picnic" means being present at one.`}},{id:`b2-wb-242`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`With two people we use "the more".`,bank:[`much`,`most`,`more`],text:`Of the two sisters, Anna is the ___ talkative.`,answers:[`more`],wrongReasons:{most:`With two people the comparative is used.`,much:`"Much" does not form a comparative.`}},{id:`b2-wb-243`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"In reply to" is the fixed expression.`,bank:[`at`,`in`,`on`],text:`I'm writing ___ reply to your letter of 3 May.`,answers:[`in`],wrongReasons:{on:`"On reply" is not standard.`,at:`"At reply" is not standard.`}},{id:`b2-wb-244`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Too much" goes with uncountable nouns.`,bank:[`much`,`few`,`many`],text:`He drinks too ___ coffee, and he can't sleep.`,answers:[`much`],wrongReasons:{many:`"Coffee" is uncountable.`,few:`"Few" is for countable nouns.`}},{id:`b2-wb-245`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"An" is used before the vowel sound of "unusual".`,bank:[`the`,`a`,`an`],text:`He's got ___ unusual hobby: he collects old maps.`,answers:[`an`],wrongReasons:{a:`"A" is used before consonant sounds.`,the:`"The" would refer to a known hobby.`}},{id:`b2-wb-246`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Each other" is a reciprocal pronoun.`,bank:[`each other`,`one`,`themselves`],text:`They have known ___ for many years.`,answers:[`each other`],wrongReasons:{themselves:`"Themselves" is reflexive.`,one:`"One" does not fit here.`}},{id:`b2-wb-247`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Good at" is followed by -ing.`,bank:[`at`,`on`,`in`],text:`She's very good ___ solving problems.`,answers:[`at`],wrongReasons:{in:`"Good in" is not standard here.`,on:`"Good on" is not standard here.`}},{id:`b2-wb-248`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Praised for" introduces the reason.`,bank:[`about`,`of`,`for`],text:`The students were praised ___ their hard work.`,answers:[`for`],wrongReasons:{of:`"Praised of" is not correct.`,about:`"Praised about" is not standard.`}},{id:`b2-wb-249`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"A long way" describes distance.`,bank:[`way`,`road`,`path`],text:`It's a long ___ from here to the station, so take a taxi.`,answers:[`way`],wrongReasons:{road:`A "road" is a surface.`,path:`A "path" is a narrow track.`}},{id:`b2-wb-250`,type:`word-bank`,level:`B2`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Complete the sentence with words from the box.`,explanation:`"Made of" is used when the material is still recognisable.`,bank:[`of`,`with`,`from`],text:`The ceiling is made ___ glass and steel.`,answers:[`of`],wrongReasons:{from:`"Made from" is used when the material has changed completely.`,with:`"Made with" is not standard here.`}}]};export{e as default};